## Use Case 1 for eu-ehds:PatientSummary
Description: This example presents the Primary Use application of the eu-ehds:PatientSummary by
integrating precision medicine into clinical workflows. The activity captures a data handling event
where a practitioner accesses the patient summary to ensure that a new treatment plan is compatible with the patient’s biological profile. The 
model integrates pd:Genetic data to identify hereditary predispositions that influence drug efficacy and pd:Physical data to assess the 
patient's current physiological capacity to metabolise specific compounds. The processing is anchored in dpv:PublicInterest, reflecting the 
mandate of the European Health Data Space to utilise standardised health information for the prevention of adverse medical events and the 
enhancement of public health outcomes. To safeguard this highly sensitive information, dpv:Deidentification masks the raw biological 
sequences during the initial screening phase and enforces dpv:AccessControlMethod to ensure that only authorided clinical staff 
can view detailed results. The adherence to a dpv:TechnicalStandard ensures that these clinical insights are fully interoperable,
allowing the eu-ehds:PatientSummary to function as a safety tool following the patient across the European healthcare ecosystem.

## Use Case 2 for eu-ehds:MedicalDevicesPrescription and eu-ehds:MedicalDevicesProvision
Description: This example presents a Primary Use scenario within the European Health Data Space (EHDS) framework, 
focusing on the clinical workflow of prescribing and delivering medical devices. It models a dpv:PersonalDataHandling 
activity where a healthcare provider dpv:DataController manages the transition from a clinical prescription to the physical 
provision and technical calibration of a medical device. The process is grounded in dpv:OfficialAuthorityOfController, reflecting 
the clinician's mandated duty to provide healthcare services and the state’s role in health system management under the EHDS. 
The system integrates dpv:PrivacyByDesign to ensure that data protection is embedded into the clinical workflow mandating that 
only the minimum necessary data is shared for a specific treatment. This organisational policy is enforced through robust technical 
measures dpv:EncryptionAtRest which protects the sensitive eu-ehds:PersonalGeneratedMedicalDeviceData from unauthorised access during 
storage, and dpv:AccessControlMethod restricting data interaction to authorised clinical staff and technical engineers, ensuring a clear
and secure audit trail. Aligning the purposes of eu-ehds:MedicalDevicesPrescription and eu-ehds:MedicalDevicesProvision, the example 
demonstrates how the EHDS standardises data flows between clinics and manufacturers,allowing for the customised delivery of devices calibrated
to the patient's specific physiological needs.

## Use Case 3 for eu-ehds:DischargeReport
Description:This use case illustrates the digital handover of health data following a cardiac procedure, modeling the eu-ehds:DischargeReport 
as a priority category required by the European Health Data Space. The process involves a sector-health:HealthcareProvider which acts as the 
dpv:DataController, who prepares and shares a structured clinical summary with a ex:PrimaryCareSpecialist to ensure care continuity. 
Leveraging the pd: extension further helps the system to distinguish between pd:MedicalHealth, which includes formal surgical codes and diagnoses,
and pd:PhysicalHealth documenting the patient's actual recovery status and vital signs. This is supplemented by the pd:IndividualHealthHistory to 
provide a chronological account of the entity's hospital stay. To protect this sensitive information, the system relies on dpv:PhysicalAccessControlMethod 
to secure the clinical workstations in the ward and dpv:EncryptionAtRest to safeguard the stored records. The activity reaches dpv:ActivityCompleted 
once the report is successfully transmitted, maintaining a dpv:hasComplianceStatus of dpv:Compliant. Furthermore, the application of a dpv:TechnicalStandard
ensures that the report is fully interoperable and follows the required European exchange formats. This approach eliminates information gaps during 
the care transition, directly supporting patient safety and recovery.
